This is the exercism forth exercise. Key points:

- `Forth(input []string) ([]int, error)` — each element of input is a line.
- Words are case-insensitive.
- Numbers: a sequence of one or more ASCII digits, optionally with a leading `-` (negative numbers get pushed). Per the spec: "a number is a sequence of one or more (ASCII) digits" — but the tests include negative numbers. So a number is `-` followed by digits.
- Definition: `: word-name definition ;` — the body is stored as tokens (words), and when called, the body is executed with the current word table (so `: foo foo 1 + ;` works — the body references the *new* foo).
- User-defined words are stored as a list of tokens to re-execute.
- `: 1 2 ;` is an error (illegal operation).
- Unknown word is an error.
- Stack errors: empty stack / only one value.

Note: "cannot redefine negative numbers" — `-1` is a number, so defining it is an error.

